WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.260
It’s Professor Dave, let’s learn the distance
formula.

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:15.440
As we navigate the coordinate plane in our
algebraic adventures, one thing we might want

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:18.610
to do is find the distance between two points.

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:24.530
Luckily, we are well equipped to do this,
since we know the Pythagorean Theorem.

00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:29.840
If you need to review it, check out the tutorials
on how to use the theorem, as well as how

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:34.559
the theorem was derived, in the geometry portion
of this playlist.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:41.880
Otherwise, let’s just select two points
in the coordinate plane, and draw a line between them.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.409
The length of this line is what we are trying
to figure out, and in order to get it, we

00:00:46.409 --> 00:00:48.699
can make a right triangle.

00:00:48.699 --> 00:00:54.440
This line will be the hypotenuse, and the
legs will be the horizontal and vertical line

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:57.629
segments we need to construct the triangle.

00:00:57.629 --> 00:01:02.570
Now let’s label the hypotenuse as D, the
distance we want to know.

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:08.720
The legs can be expressed as the difference
between the respective points that mark their ends.

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:14.320
This horizontal line segment has a length
of X two minus X one, since it only moves

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:15.950
in the X direction.

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:21.010
The vertical line segment has a length of
Y two minus Y one, since it only moves in

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:22.240
the Y direction.

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:25.600
Now, we just use the Pythagorean Theorem.

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:32.940
The square of this leg plus the square of
the other leg equals the hypotenuse squared,

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:34.240
or D squared.

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:39.300
Then, all we do is take the square root of
both sides to solve for D.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:44.850
We will only take the positive value here,
as negative length doesn’t make any sense.

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:48.080
And there you have it, we have derived the
distance formula.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:49.930
Let’s give it a shot.

00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:54.020
Take the points negative two, two, and two,
five.

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:58.140
What is the distance covered by the line segment
that connects these two points?

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:05.480
Well, let’s take our X values and our Y
values, and plug them into the formula.

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:11.930
That gives us four, which squared is sixteen,
and three, which squared is nine.

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:18.060
Add them up and we get twenty-five, take the
square root, and that gives us five for the

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:20.170
distance.

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:25.630
This is the same value we would get if we
contructed a triangle and performed the Pythagorean

00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:26.630
Theorem.

00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:32.819
So the distance formula is a generalized way
to simply plug in the coordinates of two points,

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:34.900
and find the distance between them.

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:36.160
Let's check comprehension.

